# Trivy ignore rules — every entry MUST link to a SECURITY.md assessment.
# Format: one CVE per line; trailing comment optional but encouraged.

# CVE-2026-14257 — brace-expansion 5.0.7 DoS (unbounded expansion length)
# vendored inside the global npm@11.18.0 installed by the Dockerfile runtime
# stage. Fixed only in brace-expansion 5.0.8 (published 2026-07-23), which no
# npm release vendors yet. Not exploitable in our runtime image: the bundled
# npm is only invoked by operators running `npm run hostkeys:prod` against
# first-party package.json scripts — no attacker-controlled glob patterns
# reach it. See SECURITY.md → "CVE-2026-14257 (brace-expansion DoS in bundled
# npm)". Re-evaluate on each npm release: bump the pinned npm version in the
# Dockerfile once a release vendors brace-expansion >= 5.0.8, then remove
# this entry.
CVE-2026-14257

# CVE-2026-69152 (GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895) — brace-expansion 5.0.7 DoS via
# unbounded intermediate arrays (bypass of the CVE-2026-14257 mitigation).
# Same vendored copy inside the global npm@11.18.0 as above; fixed in 5.0.9
# (published 2026-07-30), which no npm release vendors yet (checked 11.19.0,
# 2026-08-18). Same non-exploitability rationale as CVE-2026-14257. See
# SECURITY.md → "CVE-2026-69152 (brace-expansion DoS bypass in bundled npm)".
CVE-2026-69152

# CVE-2026-69192 (GHSA-mwp4-54f8-5fhr) — ip-address 10.2.0 inconsistent
# leading-zero octet parsing (SSRF / trust-boundary bypass). Vendored inside
# the global npm@11.18.0 via socks-proxy-agent → socks → ip-address; only
# exercised when npm itself is configured to use a SOCKS proxy. The
# application never loads code from the global npm install and no
# attacker-controlled addresses reach it. Fixed in 10.3.1 (published
# 2026-07-25), not yet vendored by any npm release. See SECURITY.md →
# "CVE-2026-69192 (ip-address SSRF in bundled npm)". Remove both entries when
# the Dockerfile npm pin is bumped to a release vendoring the fixes.
CVE-2026-69192
